/// A parameter may declare a default and be omitted at the call.
///
/// Neither ':=' nor '=' is accepted in a parameter list today; both fail with
/// 'Expect '')'' after parameters.'
///
/// ⚠️ ':=' rather than '=' because '=' is EQUALITY here.  Delphi spells a
/// default with '=', but Delphi also spells a constant that way; this language
/// writes 'const D := ...' and 'var X : Integer := 1', so ':=' is what a
/// binding looks like and '=' is what a question looks like.
///
/// ⚠️ A default is evaluated on EACH call that omits it, in the scope of the
/// declaration.  Evaluating once at declaration would hand every call the same
/// object, which is a trap worth designing out rather than documenting -- the
/// test below cannot see the difference, but a default of [] would.
///
/// Fails with: Expect ')' after parameters.  (file rejected -- no test ran)
///
function Slice (Text : String, Start : Integer := 0, Count : Integer := 1) : String;
begin
    Exit Copy (Text, Start, Count);
end

test 'A parameter default may be omitted at the call';
begin
    AssertEqual (Str ('h'),   Slice (Str ('hello')));
    AssertEqual (Str ('he'),  Slice (Str ('hello'), 0, 2));
    AssertEqual (Str ('ell'), Slice (Str ('hello'), 1, 3));
end
